00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:35.020
So before we dive into the
network architecture of Unity

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:38.590
and GCP, I want to run
through some characteristics

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:43.730
and requirements of
our infrastructure.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.690
So we have a hybrid
infrastructure.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.360
So we have some small
footprint in data center

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.810
physical location and most
of our workload running

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.570
in public cloud.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.720
And our infrastructure
is not only

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.580
span across multiple region.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:04.420
It's also span across
multiple clouds.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:08.020
Fiber backbone is
primarily IPsec based.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:11.570
But for some workload
between certain environments

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:15.580
we do have the requirement
to have much higher capacity

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:20.050
bandwidth requirement where
multiple IPsec panels are not

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.610
going to meet the requirement.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.030
And also our infrastructure
have very high growth rate.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:34.310
So our design in GCP
need to be able to scale.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:38.470
So with that in mind, when
we start our journey in GCP,

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:43.450
start to build in 2018, we
work with the Google team

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:47.470
and quickly come up with a set
of key architecture decisions.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:52.350
Those decisions then later
on play the key factor in

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:58.200
to determine the final
GCP network build.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.580
So first, we work
with the Google team.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.630
We decided to use one
VPC for transiting

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.290
routing between GCP and the
other cloud infrastructure

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.490
we have.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.650
And also we know
that, especially

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.920
during the migration, we will
need some partner interconnect

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.650
based product to link
between the environment,

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:22.140
certain environments, to
provide much higher bandwidth.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.680
And also we decided
to use global routing

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.720
versus regional routing.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.720
The reason being is because
with the global routing

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.809
we will be able to do
failover between regions,

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:37.230
hence increase our
network availability.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.900
Also we decided to
use Cloud Routers.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.970
So our team do
have the experience

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:47.430
to use over the shelf virtual
routing and encryption

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.420
equipment.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.960
And also we have the experience
of manage and maintain

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:56.050
Linux-based open source routing
and encryption processes.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.170
However, we choose
to use Cloud Router

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.210
because it's purely
API driven and it makes

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:03.060
our automation super easy.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.420
And also we decided
to put some workload

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.860
into shared VPC architecture.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.980
The reason to use
shared VPC architecture

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:19.650
is because use the architecture
it allows us to aggregate all

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.880
our workload into a
fewer VPC, hence reduce

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.605
the ongoing operational cost.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:34.950
So equipped with those key
decisions, we dive into the PLC

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.570
pretty quickly.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.170
And there are many
lessons learned.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:44.010
And there's something
specifically related

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:48.050
with the routing I want to
share with the audience here.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.690
So the first snag we hit when
we tried to do transit VPC

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.500
is the interoperability
between CSRs in AWS

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.250
and the cloud routers in GCP.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.240
So the CSRs in AWS are
behind the net IP address.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.080
So by default, we were
trying to negotiate--

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.360
do that IKE negotiation, it
will use the VM's private IP

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.430
as the local identity.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.530
And that caused the cloud--

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:20.560
not cloud router--
the VPN in Google site

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.390
to reject IKE negotiation.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.910
So the fix is fairly simple.

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.930
The fix is to have
one line of command

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.900
in Cisco CSR and
the IKE where you

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.400
can specify to use the public
net IP of the Cisco CSR

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:41.120
as the local identity
for IKE negotiation.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.080
So with that, we continued to
look into the routing aspect

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.780
of the transit VPC.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.660
For routing, we
used Cloud Router.

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:56.780
Cloud router for me is like the
VTW in AWS world in the sense

00:10:56.780 --> 00:11:01.010
that it provided functionality
to allow dynamic routing change

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:04.100
using BGP as well as
you have the ability

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.140
to propagate the route--

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:10.520
to program the route
into the VPC or subnet.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:15.060
However, there is one key
behavior difference there.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.060
So if Cloud Router,
they don't really

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:21.860
automatically redistribute
the best route, the BGP route,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:25.160
between the BGP peers.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.690
And also we notice that as
we learn more about GCP,

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:33.110
we realized we need to
do route summarization.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:36.020
Reason being is there
are certain limits

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.510
you need to be aware of.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.290
There is a limit about
how many prefixes you can

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.420
announce through a BGP peer.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:48.620
And there is a limit on how
many unique prefixes you

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:53.940
can program into a VPC in GCP.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:58.640
So to do route summarization,
we choose to use custom routes.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.020
And you can do custom routes
on the per cloud router level

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.090
or per VPN level--

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.790
the BGP peer level.

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:11.570
And also while we
start to experiment

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:15.890
and doing PLC about
this transit VPC idea,

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:17.720
we learned that there
is a need for two

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:21.590
set of cloud routers in each
region for transitive routing.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:25.740
To give more color on that, so
this is a simplified diagram

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.370
of the initial POC
where we will have

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:32.720
one's cloud routers in
the middle portion, which

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.130
is a transit VPC.

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:38.480
That is cloud router
have a BGP relationship

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.730
with the CSRs in AWS.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:43.070
And also it have
BGP relationship

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:45.620
with the environment
of one VPC, which

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.620
we call it downstream VPC.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.260
And that Cloud Router
intransitive VPC

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:55.790
will learn a prefix one
from AWS from the CSR.

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:58.160
And we use custom
routes and hope

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:00.920
that the customer routes
will then be re-advertised

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.540
into environment one VPC.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.700
But that's not the case.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.090
So in the end, we have
to use two cloud routers.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:11.877
So in the transit VPC,
we have cloud router one

00:13:11.877 --> 00:13:13.430
and cloud router two.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:17.360
Cloud router one is used
to receive the prefix

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:18.890
from the CSR.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:19.640
And then the cloud

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:24.500
Router will then program the
route into the transit VPC

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.880
routing table.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.380
And cloud router two--

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.290
and cloud router two will
then use custom route

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:34.970
to announce the prefix one into
the environment one VPC Cloud

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:36.680
Router.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:38.630
By doing routing
this way, we'll all

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.710
be able to do transit routing
between AWS and the environment

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:42.590
in one VPC.

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:53.250
So now here is the diagram shows
other routing with more detail.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.140
So the middle section
is the transit VPC.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.580
And in the transit VPC,
we have cloud routers

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.910
in different regions.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:04.910
In each region, we have a
set of cloud routers for one

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:08.490
plus one redundancy for
connectivity and exchange

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.750
routes with the CSRs in AWS.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.590
And also we have a set
of transit cloud routers

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:19.730
for route exchange with the
environment with VPC cloud

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:21.680
routers there.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:26.300
And also within the transit
VPC, we use global routing.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:29.780
With global routing enabled,
each of the global route--

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.280
the cloud router will
have a global route

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.550
view of the whole VPC.

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:40.100
What it means that if, for
example, we have the BGP or BGP

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:44.360
fail within region one, and the
workload in the environment one

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.450
VPC will still be able to
reroute it through the nearest

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:50.540
region to reach AWS.

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:53.720
And also here I want you
all to see the bowtie style

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.490
of connectivity.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:59.960
I want to stress that it
only represents the control

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:02.570
path for routing selection.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:06.510
So for actual data paths, it
goes through the VPN gateway.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:10.640
So for VPN gateway, we can
scale horizontally as we need.

00:15:18.592 --> 00:15:21.090
Sorry.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:25.170
And now let's look into
how we do networking

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.850
inside environment VPC.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.610
So we use share VPC.

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.700
In this diagram on the top,
you will see a share VPC

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.860
in a hosted project.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.770
In that shared VPC, you will
see that we have cloud routers

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:39.970
in many different regions.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.660
And then from that cloud
router, we have connectivity

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:46.790
to the upstream transit VPC.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:49.340
And also in the
shared VPC, we create

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:52.340
a subnet in different
region and then

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:55.640
we share the permission
to use the subnet

00:15:55.640 --> 00:16:00.710
with the maintainer of different
resources in the service

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:02.310
projects.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:03.890
So for our
development team, they

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:08.360
know if they get a subnet
from us from the shared VPC,

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:10.580
if access control
permits, they will

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.580
be able to reach to the
outer workload regardless

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:16.610
of which region
that workload is in

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.890
and which infrastructure
that workload is in.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:29.040
So this is how we do
networking in Unity in GCP.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.170
But there are many
other options that you

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:33.440
can use to connect
different VPCs

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.400
and infrastructure together.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:39.030
So next, Davis, do you want
to go through those options?

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:40.900
DAVIS PYNADATH: Absolutely.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.970
Thanks Ariel for walking
us through Unity's network

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:46.280
architecture as well
as highlighting a few

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.770
of the caveats you mentioned.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.585
Now at this point, I want
us to take a step back,

00:16:51.585 --> 00:16:53.960
because I want to highlight
what are the different design

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:58.120
options for interconnecting
VPCs in Google Cloud.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.000
So Unity leveraged VPNs.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:05.480
But based on a use case, you
could leverage different design

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:05.980
options.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:11.079
For each option, I'm going
to highlight the advantages

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:15.280
and constrictions,
And hopefully this

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:17.020
should help you make
the right decision

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:19.760
as to how to interconnect VPCs.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.589
So when you leverage VPNs, the
major advantages that it gives

00:17:23.589 --> 00:17:26.230
is it enables transitive
topologies if you are

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.850
using static cloud based VPNs.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:31.210
And if you need dynamic
cloud based VPNs,

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.660
you will have to use two cloud
routers as mentioned earlier

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.180
by Ariel.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:40.120
Secondly, you can scale
the number of VPN tunnels

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.650
if you need higher bandwidth.

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:44.470
And also the choice
of the tunnel

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.710
is based on a phi double hash.

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:51.250
It is a managed service and
has an SLA of three lines.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:54.790
Now what are the
constrictions with VPNs?

00:17:54.790 --> 00:18:00.760
Each VPN tunnel has about 1.5
gigs over public internet.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:05.020
So you can, of course, scale
with adding more tunnels.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:08.030
And also you need to account
for the IPsec headers.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:08.530
Right.

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:11.380
So on the remote end on
your on prem router maybe,

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:15.850
you have to configure [? 14 ?]
60 bytes for your ethernet MTU.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:18.700
This results in a decrease
in the maximum segment size

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.410
and hence less good [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.360
Something to be aware of.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.960
And finally, I'm sure at
least few you in the audience

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:27.700
if you have ever
created an IPsec

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:31.540
tunnel would have troubleshooted
IKE phase 1, phase 2

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:32.440
negotiation.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:38.550
So there is a little bit
of overhead and management

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.970
constraints.

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:43.160
So the next approach
for interconnecting VPCs

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:46.300
is by leveraging a
multi-NIC VM where

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:51.970
each network interface goes to
a distinct VPC in the project.

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:56.160
So this is typical as
illustrated in this diagram.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.965
So this is a design
option that you

00:18:59.965 --> 00:19:02.090
would leverage when you
need all your traffic to go

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:05.300
to an appliance like a next
generation firewall that

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.620
does deep packet inspection.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:11.490
Now with this approach, what
are the major advantages?

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:16.320
So each core of a CPU of the
VM has a peak performance

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:17.850
of two gigs.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:21.300
And if you leverage eight
cores, you get 16 gigs.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:25.320
But adding more than 8 won't
give you higher throughput.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.480
but this is again
going to change.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:35.090
You can also scale to manage
instance group and ECMP grants.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.010
The major
[? constrations ?] involved

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:41.000
that each VPN can have a maximum
of eight NICs, meaning you

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:45.020
can interconnect
maximum eight VPCs.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:49.570
All these VPCs must belong
in the same project.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:53.460
And today load balancing
is only supported on nic0.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:58.000
And for non-nic0, you would have
to leverage GCP static routes

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:59.380
and ECMP across them.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.450
That is by having
multiple static outs

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:02.740
with equal priority.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.880
The final approach for
interconnecting VPCs

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:11.480
is by leveraging VPC
peering where you peer

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:15.100
to VPCs using Google's STMP.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:18.940
So these VPCs can be
in different projects

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.100
in the same organization
or it could be

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:24.380
across different organization.

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:29.050
So here is VPC A peering
with VPC B in the diagram.

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:32.830
So the major advantages is
it's very high bandwidth.

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:33.370
Right.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:37.465
It's as if all the VPCs in the
peering group is a single VPC.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:41.610
It's very easy configuration.

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:45.730
All you need to state is
the project ID as well as

00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:48.080
the remote VPC you
want to peer with.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.420
And that's pretty much it.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:52.530
And you have general
control as to the number

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:56.310
of-- as to the custom routes,
the dynamic and static routes,

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:58.160
you want to import and export.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.720
And what are the
major constrictions.

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:02.560
So it is non-transitive.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:03.810
So if you take--

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:09.030
if VPC A pairs with B and C, B
and C cannot communicate with

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:09.880
each other.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:13.420
They have to be
directly connected.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:15.460
Also something to
be aware of when

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:20.290
you operate at very large scale
is that certain resource quotes

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:24.400
are aggregated across all the
VPCs in the peering group.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.530
Just to name a few is the VM
count and internal forwarding

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:30.210
nodes.

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:34.430
So I hope highlighting the
advantages and constrictions

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:36.290
will help you make
better decision

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:40.310
choices with your
network design in GCP.

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:44.960
Now coming back to
Unity's network journey,

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:48.500
certain key efforts
were taken by the teams

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:53.150
to onboard internal customers
into a completely new platform.

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:56.990
Also, tooling was developed
by the infra team at Unity

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:01.430
in order to easily enable
customers to use a shared VPC.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:03.770
And I'll have Ariel
to speak more on this.

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:09.010
ARIEL WEI: All right.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:14.730
So after we built the network,
we spent a lot of effort

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:19.570
to onboard our
internal customers.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:20.850
And it's a big effort.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:24.360
And it's effort actually across
many different teams, not just

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:25.380
us.

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.540
So first we ran a
lot of onboarding

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.250
related presentations.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:31.410
We have the presentation
with different customers

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:32.700
in different regions.

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.670
And in the
presentation, we focused

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:37.500
on describing to
the customer what's

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:40.590
the new architecture
in GCP, what's

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.592
the characteristics
of our network,

00:22:43.592 --> 00:22:45.050
such as what's the
bandwidth that's

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:48.330
supported, what they can
expect in terms of bandwidth

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:51.060
between different environments,
what's the latency,

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:55.560
and what's the service level
objectives that we use to come

00:22:55.560 --> 00:23:00.030
up with the network design.

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:02.850
And those informations are very
important for our development

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:07.200
teams to be able to eventually
come up with their migration

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:10.410
strategy that can be
supported by the underlying

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:14.190
infrastructure.

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:17.900
We also created a
GCP kickstart guide.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:22.040
It's a guide that provided
step by step instructions

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:27.290
on how to start in GCP, such
as how to create a project, how

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:30.110
to access to the
project, how to be

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:33.215
able to share the permissions
with the other team members.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:39.670
And we also created a
portal for project creation.

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:42.000
It's not as simple as
just to create a project.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:45.570
It's actually a
suite of actions.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:47.020
It will create a project.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:51.300
And if the project requires
to use the shared VPC,

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:54.810
it will then be attached
to our host project.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:58.320
And also it will delete things
like the default network

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:04.660
and create relevant
service accounts.

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:09.470
And we also create a portal
for firewall rule request.

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.920
It's a GUI driven portal.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:15.280
And turns out we didn't
have the very high adoption

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:20.260
rate as we hoped, because
most of our developers

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:24.100
like to write infrastructure
in code although it

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:29.720
proved to be very useful to
some teams and some people.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:33.470
And then we decided to
share our Terraform repos

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:38.850
on subnets and firewall rules
with the development team.

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:44.570
And to do that, we used
Terraform modules whenever

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:49.380
that makes sense to enforce a
certain standard and templates.

00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:53.120
And also we found it's really
useful to install some linters.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.670
The linters we installed in
our platform CD pipeline,

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:02.930
it allows us to be able
to verify and qualify

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:07.040
a merger request
before a human come

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:08.450
to review the merger request.

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:12.080
What it does is,
for example, it will

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:17.210
check if the format is right,
if there is any syntax errors,

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:21.950
or if there are any variables
used that are not declared.

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:24.680
Also, it will check, for
example, for firewall rules.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:26.510
It will check if
the firewall rule

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:31.430
name is more than 63 character,
which typically you will only

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:34.340
notice that at the time
of the terraform apply

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.200
and it will be able
to expose to you

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:39.440
because Google API
will give you an error.

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:44.030
But with that linter, we can
detect those errors upfront.

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:46.970
And using these
methods and tactics

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:49.250
that it help us to
onboard our customers

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:52.160
span across different
locations without us

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.610
being the bottleneck.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:57.200
As we onboard
customer, we also start

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:00.230
to work on the network
monitoring and logging

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:02.210
because we want to
make sure we have

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:04.820
the visibility of
our network health

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:09.150
within GCP as our
internal customers start

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:12.230
to iterate within GCP.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:16.460
So we have our own in-house
monitoring implementation.

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:18.980
With the help from our
monitoring and logging team,

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:23.060
we quickly implemented
some backbone connectivity

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:23.860
monitoring.

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:27.260
It will monitor the
connectivity and the latency

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.320
and availability between
different environments

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:33.320
within GCP and outside GCP.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:35.780
We also use our
in-house monitoring

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:39.660
for metric gathering of some
VMs running network services,

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:43.160
such as network
address translation.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.280
And we also heavily utilize
a Stackdriver logging

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:48.620
and monitoring,
especially for elements

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:53.660
such as the Google VPN gateway
or Google Cloud routers.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:56.830
So speak of Stackdriver.

00:26:56.830 --> 00:26:59.290
Davis, do you want to give
us some examples on how

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:02.705
to mine the Cloud Router events
using Stackdriver logging?

00:27:02.705 --> 00:27:03.830
DAVIS PYNADATH: Absolutely.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:04.330
Yeah.

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:05.635
Thanks, Ariel.

00:27:05.635 --> 00:27:10.090
So I want to walk through a few
examples of using Stackdriver

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:12.850
logging queries to data
mine cloud routing events.

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:13.600
Right.

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.590
So the first one is
when a cloud router

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:17.860
is being upgraded or migrated.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.590
So this is usually,
since cloud router

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:23.650
is a fully managed
service, it does

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:25.580
undergo upgrades and patches.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:26.080
Right.

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:29.200
And these upgrades are completed
in less than 60 seconds.

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:31.070
The hold down timer
is 60 seconds,

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:34.030
meaning traffic
forwarding should not

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:37.150
be affected as the
routes are preserved.

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:41.250
So what you need to
look out for is--

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:42.490
not sure if it's clear.

00:27:42.490 --> 00:27:46.200
So essentially you
go to Cloud Console.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:49.320
And then you hit logging,
Stackdriver logging, logs,

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:51.720
and you convert into
an advance filter

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:54.930
where you search for
resource type GC router

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:57.900
and look for the
keyword task activated.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:01.555
So this is something
that you should track.

00:28:01.555 --> 00:28:03.180
This is an event that
you should track.

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:08.880
Next is something, an event of
when a BGP peering goes down.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.130
So this is essentially
a network outage

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:14.580
if you don't have
redundancy in place.

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:17.265
So a critical event
to look out for.

00:28:17.265 --> 00:28:22.020
What happens is you need to look
for the keywords BGP peering

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:23.880
and went down.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.930
And as you can see
here, the neighbor 1.6

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:32.080
has gone down at that
particular timestamp.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:34.820
And immediately after
the neighbor goes down,

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:37.595
well, they're also going to
be evicted from the VPC table.

00:28:37.595 --> 00:28:40.290
And what you look
for in this case

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:47.770
is the Route Event and
deleted from the cloud router.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:51.870
So as you can see here,
10.2.0.0/24 is being evicted

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:55.740
from the same router whose
peering session went down

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:58.680
earlier from the earlier slide.

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.960
So this is, essentially,
I just wanted

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:04.350
to give you a glimpse of how
you could use Stackdriver log

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:05.610
inquiries.

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:07.800
Now, what do you do with these?

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:12.630
So you could export it into
Pub/Sub and a Pub/Sub topic.

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:15.630
And as these events
occur, you could

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:17.400
trigger a Cloud
Function that executes

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:19.586
an API for [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:25.000
So that is kind of the
capability of Stackdriver.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:29.110
And, well, I now
want to kind of focus

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.000
on a particular
network service, which

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:33.370
is Network Address Translation.

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:36.390
The reason I bring this
up is because there

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:39.040
are a few considerations
with its implementation.

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:43.760
And I believe it will be useful
to you to be aware of it.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:46.890
So, firstly, there
are two approaches

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:49.300
to implementing NAT in GCP.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.310
The first one is
self-managed NAT

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:53.890
where you run a VM
with NAT software

00:29:53.890 --> 00:29:59.450
or maybe a Linux VM doing IP
tables for NAT functionality.

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:02.800
So what are the considerations
with this approach?

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:06.190
You need to monitor the VM
for scale as well as for load.

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:09.580
You need to create certain
scripts to do this.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:12.460
Second, I cannot
stress enough on this,

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:14.320
it breaks Google Private Access.

00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:16.240
So, now, what is Private Access?

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:20.080
It's basically private
VMs having the capability

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:24.070
to make GCP API
calls and services

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:25.720
through Google's backbone.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:28.600
This is made possible
using a default route

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:31.700
with the next stop as
default internet gateway.

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:36.490
So when you override that route,
and when you create a default

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:38.500
route with next stop
as NAT instance,

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:41.520
all your traffic is going
to go through NAT instance,

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:43.960
and it overloads the NAT,
and it might bring down

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:45.340
the NAT instance.

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:47.880
So I've been on many
troubleshooting calls

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:48.880
essentially fixing this.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:53.890
So yeah, it's an
important caveat to note.

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:57.030
Next is the same point
I mentioned earlier

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:59.650
with multi-NIC VMs.

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:02.670
Each vCPU has about 2
gigs peak performance.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:05.950
And as you use [INAUDIBLE]
you get 16 gigs

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:09.490
as max for peak performance.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:10.690
Now, what is Cloud NAT?

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:12.380
And how does this help at all?

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:14.780
Well, it is
Google-managed service.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:16.480
It has an SLA of [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:20.290
It is highly available,
as well as [INAUDIBLE],,

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:24.760
without any user
intervention or management.

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:27.040
The perks of running
a managed service.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:30.220
Now, Private Google Access
automatically works,

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:35.290
and your bandwidth is
as of your private VMS

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:39.010
that need to be NATed
having an external IP.

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:42.650
So, essentially, huge
performance benefits.

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:43.870
Considerations, though.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:48.115
Cloud NAT does not support
application-level gateways.

00:31:48.115 --> 00:31:52.290
What this means is certain
protocols, like SIP and FTP,

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.610
they require the IP
in the application

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:56.950
PDU to be NATed also.

00:31:56.950 --> 00:32:00.580
So Cloud NAT does not have
that functionality today.

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:02.220
Logging is in Alpha.

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:09.910
And also, finally, when
you create Cloud NAT,

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:14.320
you have to specify the
maximum number of ports

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:16.500
which are available to each VM.

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:21.380
By default, it's 64 ports.

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:24.310
So if you say 64 ports
are available to each VM

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:29.020
for [? NATing ?] purposes,
and the VM essentially

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:31.660
makes a large number
of connections

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:37.970
to the same destination IP
and port, which is always 64,

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:41.920
then the packet is
not going to be NATed.

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:45.640
So you need to kind
of plan what subnets

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:54.040
or what workstreams need to be
made available for Cloud NAT.

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:57.250
And I think Ariel will
share her experience

00:32:57.250 --> 00:32:58.930
on its implementation at Unity.

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:08.010
ARIEL WEI: So for the
Unity NAT implementation.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:12.990
When we first started
back in early 2018,

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:15.460
our choice is very
simple, because there

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.050
is no Cloud NAT available.

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:20.010
So we created many NAT clusters.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:25.700
We have one NAT cluster in each
region and each environment.

00:33:25.700 --> 00:33:30.320
And also, we use route
tags to route route tags,

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:33.420
and then provide those tags
to our development team.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.970
So the development team
can use these tags once.

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.470
It's applied to a VM.

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:40.655
It will route the
internet traffic.

00:33:40.655 --> 00:33:45.778
When I say internet, the
traffic goes to prefix of 000/0,

00:33:45.778 --> 00:33:50.430
what we route is through
the relevant NAT cluster.

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:52.930
So it works well at
the very beginning.

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:54.720
However, later in
the migration, we

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:58.200
start to notice that,
occasionally, our NAT

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:00.215
clusters may get overwhelmed.

00:34:02.830 --> 00:34:05.530
And this is a usage graph.

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:08.230
And it shows, you can see,
that the traffic pattern

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:09.204
can be really spiky.

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:16.389
And this is only a throughput
for one environment and one

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:17.360
region we have.

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:19.360
And at times, we
will see traffic

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:22.270
goes from
4-point-something gigabit

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:27.580
per second to 40-plus gigabit
per second within five minutes.

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:31.929
And when we look deeper, we
notice majority of this traffic

00:34:31.929 --> 00:34:35.440
are actually not the traffic
go to the public internet.

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:40.150
A lot of the traffic is
actually goes to the Google API

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:44.710
services, like GCS or GCR.

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:48.260
And then, luckily,
at the time when

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:50.219
we start to
experience this issue,

00:34:50.219 --> 00:34:53.739
Cloud NAT is
already [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:57.760
But before we immediately
jump into the Cloud NAT

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:02.440
implementation, we looked at
several different options.

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:04.900
We look at option
where we can possibly

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:07.660
inject more specific
routes in the routing table

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:10.960
for certain Google APIs
to route those traffic

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:13.000
through the Google
internet gateway that

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:16.170
allows Private Google Access.

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:21.940
We also looked at these
restricted Google API access

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:24.040
feature, but we
have some concerns

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:29.800
about how to do the
DNS using that feature.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:32.960
And eventually,
after some analysis,

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:37.830
we decided Google Cloud NAT
is the direction we should go.

00:35:44.490 --> 00:35:47.220
So when we start looking
to migrate workloads

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:50.880
into Cloud NAT, the first
question we ask ourselves,

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:53.610
what are the workload we
should be focusing on in

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:55.350
migrating to Cloud NAT?

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:59.070
At the time, our NAT clusters
occasionally get overwhelmed.

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:02.590
We want to be able to
stabilize our service.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.340
So in order to find the
top talker, what we did

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:08.880
is to utilize the flow logs.

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.790
So we enabled flow logs
for the subnet, where

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:15.990
our NAT devices uses, and
then we send those flow logs

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:21.390
into BigQuery, and with
a fairly simple query,

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:23.760
we will be able to
find the top talkers.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:27.510
So identify the top talkers
allows us to figure out

00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:31.830
which development team we
then should collaborate with

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:36.300
to migrate those
traffic into Cloud NAT.

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:37.830
And then the second
question we ask

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:42.270
ourselves is how to
qualify if a workload is

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:45.060
a good candidate for Cloud NAT?

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:48.350
Because of the caveats that
Davis mentioned before,

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:49.980
when you create a
Cloud NAT, you do

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:53.910
have to actually put a number
for a maximum concurrent

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:58.290
connection a VM can
establish to a destination

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:01.720
IP and a port combination.

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:06.000
So what we don't want is
to migrate something, jump

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:07.890
into Cloud NAT,
migrate to Cloud NAT,

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:12.060
and potentially have workload
experience [INAUDIBLE] failures

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:15.100
because of the port exhaustion.

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:20.670
So what we did is to
first mine the flow

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:22.950
logs we have in the query.

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:25.470
But for some reason, we
find that we can't really

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:30.510
find all the flows that for
one particular stream before it

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:33.220
goes into the NAT, after
it come out of the NAT,

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:37.530
get NATed, and come back, and
eventually back to the VM.

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:39.390
And maybe that's
because of the sampling.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:40.740
We didn't look further.

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:43.960
But we quickly developed
another method.

00:37:43.960 --> 00:37:47.940
So our team members
start to take snapshot

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:51.330
of all the connection tracking
tables across all the NAT

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:54.270
devices within one cluster.

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:58.980
And then we have a
little simple Go client.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:02.190
That Go client basically
inject all these connection

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:05.910
tracking table, information
and parse it, and then send it,

00:38:05.910 --> 00:38:08.550
stream it into BigQuery.

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:12.600
With this method, we basically
gain the real-time visibility

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.360
into the characteristics
of all our traffic

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:18.030
that outbound to the internet.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:20.730
And this allows us
to be able to tell,

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:24.510
by looking to a subnet, normally
correlates to one workload,

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:27.420
it will tell us how many
concurrent connections,

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:29.790
the maximum concurrent
connections, from the time

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:33.030
we enable the method that
can ever recorded if that

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:36.430
can be supported by Cloud NAT.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:40.740
So after we find the
top talkers and qualify

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:43.860
if they're a good
candidate to Cloud NAT,

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:46.500
then we ask ourselves
the third question.

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:50.820
How to migrate from using our
NAT devices into Cloud NAT

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:54.120
with minimum service impact?

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:59.640
So we choose to enable Cloud
NAT on a per subnet basis.

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:01.740
And also, once we
enable the Cloud NAT

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:06.630
for that particular subnet, then
it's up to our development team

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:09.990
to remove the NAT
tag at their pace

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:13.530
whenever they're ready to
migrate into Cloud NAT.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:15.930
Once they remove
the NAT tag, what's

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:18.420
the impact to the
existing traffic?

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.520
For the existing
connections, when

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:24.520
you switch from NAT to the
Cloud NAT, it will send a reset.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:28.620
So for the type of workload
we have, the traffic we have,

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:32.482
it's nearly not detectable
for this transition.

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:40.590
So it turns out that
to migrate to Cloud NAT

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:43.728
is actually fairly manageable.

00:39:43.728 --> 00:39:45.270
But we're still
trying to figure out,

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:48.750
how can we do capacity planning
and ongoing capacity planning,

00:39:48.750 --> 00:39:51.870
so that we can take a
more proactive position?

00:39:51.870 --> 00:39:54.190
And that is true
not only for NAT,

00:39:54.190 --> 00:39:56.520
but also that's true for
all the other services we

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:59.400
manage in GCP.

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:04.490
So now after one year in
GCP, what should we do?

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:06.340
What should we be focusing on?

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:10.220
Of course, we pop a
bottle of champagne first.

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:15.150
And then after that,
we know that we

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:18.810
need to monitor quota
and the resource usage,

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:22.920
the reason being is that
once we situated into GCP,

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:25.170
the workload continue to grow.

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:28.830
So we have to keep our eye and
have a good visibility into all

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:31.470
the quota usage
and resource usage,

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:34.800
so that we can be
more proactive.

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:38.590
And also, we need to focus
on optimizing our workload,

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:41.310
not only just for
cost optimization,

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:46.430
but also for service
performance optimization.

00:40:46.430 --> 00:40:48.870
And then we start to
have more and more ask

00:40:48.870 --> 00:40:51.180
from different
development teams where

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:54.060
they have their own self-managed
infrastructure where

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:58.320
they want to be able to
connect into GCP directly.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.620
And for example, now we
are working on a project

00:41:01.620 --> 00:41:04.440
where we will utilize
the new Hong Kong

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:07.530
region offered by
GCP for connectivity

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:10.170
and better connectivity
into our infrastructure

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:13.740
in mainland China.

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:17.850
And also, we invest very
heavily on automation.

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:20.490
We invest on automation
for the purpose

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:26.280
to have a self-service
approach for our developers,

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:30.690
so that they can iterate
really fast rather than create

00:41:30.690 --> 00:41:33.990
a request and wait for
us to do the request.

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:38.040
And also, for the purpose
of reduced operational cost.

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:40.620
And also, speak
from the experience.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:44.340
I think public cloud
technology evolved very fast.

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:48.580
And also, Google release new
features on a regular basis.

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:50.490
So it's always
something that we need

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:54.570
to keep informed on the new
features and new product,

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:59.640
and reevaluate our architecture
to ensure that we can

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:03.750
meet the future growth needs.

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:08.690
So this is our network team's
focus after the migration.

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:11.960
And, Davis, you interact
with many other customers.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:14.557
Do you want to add
anything else here?

00:42:14.557 --> 00:42:15.890
DAVIS PYNADATH: Yes, absolutely.

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:17.810
Thanks, Ariel.

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:21.650
So, one, as Ariel
mentioned earlier,

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:23.540
carefully monitor your quotas.

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:27.890
So these quotas are present
to prevent abuse of a platform

00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:33.560
as well as to protect you
from unforeseen usages.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:36.170
So there are two kinds
of quotas, rate quotas,

00:42:36.170 --> 00:42:39.710
which get reset maybe every day,
maybe every minute, depending

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:41.570
on the API you're calling.

00:42:41.570 --> 00:42:43.250
There's also the
resource quotas,

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:46.220
which is essentially like a
VM [INAUDIBLE] project, which

00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:47.780
doesn't get reset.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:52.550
So you need to carefully
monitor and proactively request

00:42:52.550 --> 00:42:55.640
for quota increases, because
depending on your resource,

00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:57.130
it does take time for approval.

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:02.707
Second, so we are at Next.

00:43:02.707 --> 00:43:04.790
There are a lot of product
announcements happening

00:43:04.790 --> 00:43:08.750
this week to test it out and
provide feedback, so that we

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:10.190
can quickly iterate over it.

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:18.080
Also, for the existing
products today, there

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:22.730
is a platform in which
you can essentially

00:43:22.730 --> 00:43:24.740
file issues and
feature requests,

00:43:24.740 --> 00:43:28.130
so that if you feel certain
products should behave

00:43:28.130 --> 00:43:31.280
differently or does not have a
supported functionality, well,

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:32.400
you can use this.

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:34.650
So this is kind of
how it looks like.

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:36.977
It's a public page,
and you just needed

00:43:36.977 --> 00:43:38.880
it to mine the component.

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:42.540
And if there's already an
issue that you want to file,

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:44.870
and if it already
exists, [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:44.870 --> 00:43:48.490
so that more eyes
are looking at it.

00:43:48.490 --> 00:43:52.670
Finally, for your favorite
GCP products, bookmark it.

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:56.450
Example is, well, here is
Cloud NAT maturing from beta

00:43:56.450 --> 00:44:00.300
[INAUDIBLE] last year.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:04.640
And finally, we
continuously strive

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:10.280
to expand our regions globally.

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:13.580
And, well, as you
mentioned, we do

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:17.750
have a region in Hong
Kong, which is asia-east2.

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:22.100
So if you have queries
on accessibility of users

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:24.860
to GCP resources,
like accessibility

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:27.940
of users in China
to GCP resources,

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:30.650
well, there are
interconnectivity options.

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:33.440
We have the China specialist
team sitting in this room

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:38.180
right here or you can come
talk to me later about it, too.

00:44:38.180 --> 00:44:39.430
ANANTH SUNDARRAJAN: All right.

00:44:39.430 --> 00:44:40.660
Thanks, Davis.

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:42.520
So I hope this
experience sharing

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:45.040
that we had between
Google and Unity

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:47.950
has helped you out to
think beyond the obvious.

00:44:47.950 --> 00:44:51.160
And we do hope that while you
are planning your migration,

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:53.350
you do keep these
points in mind.

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:56.227
If you're already
